  "query_preview": "..Cloud cover led to a pleasant morning with lows in the \n60s and 70s across southeast Arizona. There were a few lingering \nshowers and storms from overnight convection. Overall, we saw a \nslowdown in the convective activity through the morning hours. Now, \nwe start to see a ramp up again with showers and storms so far \nforming mainly south and east of Tucson in the White Mountains.\n\nThe atmospheric environment this afternoon favors isolated to \nscattered storms capable of producing moderate to heavy rainfall. \nThe Weather Prediction Center has Graham, Greenlee, Cochise, Santa \nCruz, Pinal, and eastern Pima counties in a slight risk for \nexcessive rainfall that could lead to flash flooding. Hourly \nrainfall rates could be between 0.75 to 1.5 inches per hour, which \nwould be enough to result in localized instances of flash flooding.\n\nAs a upper level trough approaches the area from the west coast, \nflow will become more southwesterly promoting lift and another round \nof nocturnal showers and thunderstorms from late tonight into early \ntomorrow morning mainly north and east of Tucson. Cannot rule out \nisolated flash flooding over Graham/Greenlee and Pinal counties with \nthis activity.\n\nFor the remainder of the work week into the weekend we will see a \ngradual downturn in the coverage and intensity of showers and \nstorms. Not to say that we won't have any activity at all, but we \nstart to minimize the necessary ingredients for widespread showers \nand storms. We lose our lift provided by the upper level trough \nwhich will usher dry air aloft into the region. Any storms that do \nform will be from recycled moisture in the boundary layer (low \nlevels) of the atmosphere. The best chances (10-30%) will be mainly \nfor areas east and south of Tucson.\n\nEarly next week chances for showers and storms may return as an \neasterly wave moves over northern Mexico. This would bring some \nmoisture and dynamics back into play supporting an uptick in storm \nactivity. But we could also see the moisture slow to return and \ntemperatures warming up into 105 territory for the Tucson Metro.",
